</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Choice of the weight parameter for Guidance.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We analyze the impact of the maximum weight parameter <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{max}</annotation></semantics></math> on model performance while fixing <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{min}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{min}=1</annotation></semantics></math> and using exponential scheduling. As shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure S2 ‣ S.2.2 Analysis of Pre-training strategies ‣ S.2 More Results and Analyses ‣ Joint Learning of Pose Regression and Denoising Diffusion with Score Scaling Sampling for Category-level 6D Pose Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S2</span></a>, pose estimation accuracy improves as <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{max}</annotation></semantics></math> increases from 1 to 4 on the REAL275 dataset (<math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{max}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">x</mi></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{max}=1</annotation></semantics></math> refers to w/o Guidance). However, the performance plateaus beyond <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{max}=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">x</mi></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{max}=4</annotation></semantics></math>, with minimal or no improvements for larger values. We set <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{max}=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">x</mi></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{max}=4</annotation></semantics></math> as our default value based on these empirical results.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">S.2.4 </span>Generalization ability</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following GenPose <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#biba.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, we evaluate our model on unseen categories in REAL275, focusing on symmetric objects. As presented in Table <a href="#S2.T3" title="Table S3 ‣ S.2.4 Generalization ability ‣ S.2 More Results and Analyses ‣ Joint Learning of Pose Regression and Denoising Diffusion with Score Scaling Sampling for Category-level 6D Pose Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S3</span></a>, our method exhibits generalization capabilities comparable to those of GenPose, whereas other baseline <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#biba.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> suffers significant performance degradation. This indicates that diffusion-based models can generalize effectively to unseen object categories, particularly when they share geometric structures with the training set.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.T3" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S2.T3.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:328.6pt;height:86pt;vertical-align:-40.5pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(0.0pt,0.0pt) scale(1,1) ;">
<div id="S2.T3.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:242.5pt;height:68.8pt;vertical-align:-32.4pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-30.3pt,8.6pt) scale(0.8,0.8) ;">
<p id="S2.T3.3.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.T3.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S2.T3.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<span class="ltx_thead">
<span id="S2.T3.3.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S2.T3.3.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt"><span id="S2.T3.3.1.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Category</span></span>
<span id="S2.T3.3.1.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt"><span id="S2.T3.3.1.1.1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Method</span></span>
<span id="S2.T3.3.1.1.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S2.T3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5^{\circ}2\text{cm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn mathsize="0.900em">5</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">∘</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext mathsize="0.900em">cm</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5^{\circ}2\text{cm}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
